  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 322 AM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nMoving into the mid-late week period, the forecast reasoning has \nnot changed significantly in the last 24-48 hours.  An amplifying \nridge is expected over the western CONUS with downstream troughing \ncentered along the eastern US coast.  Locally, a northwest flow \nregime will continue across the Ohio Valley.  A stalled out frontal \nboundary will likely remain draped across or at least bisect the \nregion during this time period.  This boundary will likely stretch \nfrom central IL through central/southern IN and into eastern KY.\n\nThis boundary may slip a little more southward into KY on Wednesday \nand with a couple of perturbations moving through within the mean \nflow, more episodic bouts of thunderstorm activity are expected.  In \nareas south of the quasi-stationary front, diurnal heating will lead \nto strong instability and with shear increasing across the region, \nstrong to severe storms will be possible.  The main hazards would be \ntorrential rainfall, intense lightning, and the potential for \ndamaging winds.  Depending on where the front ends up, portions of \nwestern and possibly southern KY may remain dry and hot through the \nperiod.  Daytime highs Wed/Thu will be in the upper 80s/lower 90s \nwith overnight lows in the 70s.\n\nMoving into the late week and weekend, we're starting to see a \nstronger signal/trend in the data with ridging building over eastern \nTX and into LA/MS/AL.  If this scenario pans out, we'll see the \nstalled out frontal boundary across our region lift northward and \npush the storm track back to the north.  This would also bring \nwarmer temperatures back into the region.  Current guidance shows \nhighs in the upper 80s to around 90 for Fri-Sun across southern IN \nand the northern half of KY.  Highs in the lower-mid 90s will be \npossible across western/southern KY, especially if they remain rain-\nfree for much of this week.",
